The assertion checks that the capacity (size of the array) remains the same as the `Count` property (number of objects), which should be the case whenever the list is not resized during modifications.

<code>
Debug.Assert(RET >= 0);
</code>
The assertion checks that the index returned by the `IndexOf` method is always non-negative. The `IndexOf` method's return indicates the position of the object in the list, and -1 indicates that the object is not in the list.

<code>
Debug.Assert(RET != -1);
</code>
The assertion checks that the return value is not -1, indicating that the given object is indeed in the list.

<code>
Debug.Assert(Array.LastIndexOf((Array)_items, value, _size) == RET);
</code>
The assertion checks that the `LastIndexOf` method will return the same index as `IndexOf`, which confirms that both methods are returning the correct position of the object in the list.

It should be noted that these assert statements may not work as expected if they are used out side of the provided methods or are placed in a different context where the variables are not initialised or modified.
